FRESH READ-ONLY AUDIT OF THE BOUNDED-READER TRANSPORT SEAM (stoa.ts:148-215), as instructed. It is sound, and stronger than 39dq needs:
- Argument guards (:153-166) refuse a maximum outside [1, MAX_STOA_FELLOW_LIST_RESPONSE_BYTES] and a timeoutMs outside [1, STOA_RESPONSE_READ_TIMEOUT_MS], both with TypeError. A caller therefore cannot raise the read timeout above the 8s module ceiling — the bound is not caller-tunable upward.
- Content-length early refusal (:167-176) discards before any read, and only for canonical decimal values, so an absent or malformed header correctly falls through to streaming rather than being trusted.
- The overrun check (:198-206) refuses the chunk that WOULD exceed — if (next.value.byteLength > maximum - total) cancel and return undefined — so total never reaches maximum + 1. The per-response bound is exact, not approximate.
- Allocation is lazy: :187 starts at min(maximum, 16_384) and :207-214 doubles capped at maximum, so a 16 MiB ceiling costs 16 KiB until bytes actually arrive. This is why item 5 needs no large buffer.
- The deadline is belt-and-braces: a setTimeout-backed rejecting promise raced against each read, PLUS a performance.now() check at the top of every iteration, so an endless fast-chunk stream still hits the wall clock.
No defect found in the seam. It is the reason the compositional bound is real; what 39dq lacks is only protection against the two factors being changed, which items 1-5 supply.

